course: biology
lesson: 254
title: Plants deeper: carnivorous plants that count, slip and suck
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most plants get what they need from sunlight, air, water and soil, but some live in wet bogs where the soil has almost no nitrogen, the stuff plants need to build themselves.
explain: These plants found a trick: they catch insects and digest them, the way you might take vitamins, while still making their main food from sunlight like any other green plant.
explain: The Venus flytrap is a plant that counts. Inside its trap are tiny trigger hairs, and only when two of them are touched within about 20 seconds does it snap shut, so a raindrop or a falling leaf does not fool it.
explain: Other hunters have other tricks: pitcher plants are slippery water slides into a pool of juice, sundews are covered in sticky drops, and underwater bladderworts suck in water fleas like tiny vacuum cleaners.
explain: The giant man-eating plant of cartoons and games does not exist; the biggest real pitchers sometimes catch a frog, a lizard or, very rarely, a rat.
fact: A Venus flytrap shuts in a fraction of a second when two trigger hairs inside the trap are touched within about 20 seconds.
fact: After it shuts, the trap needs about five more touches from the struggling insect before it starts to digest, so it really counts.
fact: Each snap costs a lot of energy, a trap takes many hours to reopen, and a trap can only close a few times before it dies, so poking flytraps with a pencil harms them.
fact: Venus flytraps grow wild only in one small area of North and South Carolina in the USA.
fact: Insects slip on the wet, super slippery rim of a pitcher plant, fall in and drown in digestive juice; a lid over many pitchers keeps rain from watering the juice down.
fact: Some giant pitcher plants in Borneo are used as toilets by tree shrews, and the plant feeds on their droppings.
fact: The sparkling drops on a sundew are sticky glue that look like nectar or dew; a stuck insect is slowly wrapped by the curling tentacles.
fact: Charles Darwin wrote a whole book on insect-eating plants and tested sundews with tiny bits of meat and hair.
fact: Bladderworts have tiny underwater bladders that pump water out, so the pressure inside is low; when a water flea touches the trigger hairs, the trapdoor opens and sucks it in within about a thousandth of a second.
fact: Butterworts have flat, greasy-looking sticky leaves that catch tiny flies, and in Scandinavia their leaves were once used to thicken milk.
fact: There are more than 600 kinds of carnivorous plants, and all of them still make their food from sunlight.
fact: Stories of man-eating trees in Madagascar were a hoax printed in newspapers in the 1800s.
fact: A pet flytrap needs bright sun, wet soil, rainwater instead of tap water, whose minerals slowly harm it, a cold rest in winter, and never pieces of burger, whose fat it cannot digest, so the trap may rot.
fact: The titan arum, or corpse flower, can be over 3 metres tall and smells of rotting meat to bring in flies that pollinate it, but it eats nothing.
exercise: When does a Venus flytrap snap shut? | When two trigger hairs are touched within about 20 seconds | When any single hair is touched just once, even very lightly | When the sun goes down in the evening | When a drop of rain lands on the trap
exercise: Why does a flytrap wait for two touches before closing? | So it does not waste energy on raindrops or leaves | So the insect has time to get comfortable and stay | Because its first hair is always broken | Because it needs to count all the way to ten first
exercise: What does a flytrap do after it has shut on an insect? | It waits for about five more touches before digesting | It opens again at once to let the frightened insect go free | It digests it straight away without waiting a moment longer | It swallows the insect down into its roots under the soil
exercise: Why is it bad to keep poking a flytrap with a pencil? | Each snap uses lots of energy and a trap can close only a few times | The pencil lead poisons the whole plant at once | Flytraps feel pain like a person does | The trap will bite right through the pencil and break its own teeth off
exercise: Where do Venus flytraps grow wild? | In one small area of North and South Carolina | All over the hot rainforests of Africa and Asia | In every pond across Europe | In the deserts of Australia
exercise: How does a pitcher plant catch insects? | They slip on its wet rim and drown in the juice inside | It grabs them with sticky little hands all along its leaves | It snaps shut on them like a mouth with sharp teeth | It shoots poison darts at passing flies
exercise: What is the job of the lid on many pitcher plants? | To stop rain filling the pitcher and watering down the juice | To trap the insects inside like a door | To make a loud bang that stuns flies | To keep birds from drinking the juice inside the pitcher
exercise: What do some tree shrews in Borneo do with giant pitcher plants? | Use them as toilets, and the plant feeds on the droppings | Live inside them as a cosy nest | Eat the pitchers as their main food, all year round, every day | Use them to store nuts for winter
exercise: What are the sparkling drops on a sundew? | Sticky glue that traps insects | Fresh rainwater for the plant to drink | Sweet nectar that feeds the insects | Tiny seeds just about to fall off the leaf
exercise: Which famous scientist tested sundews with bits of meat and hair? | Charles Darwin | Isaac Newton | Albert Einstein | Marie Curie
exercise: How does a bladderwort catch water fleas? | Its bladder opens and sucks them in, in about a thousandth of a second | It slowly wraps them up in a sticky leaf and squeezes them tight until they stop | It drowns them in a little pool of juice on its flower | It spears them with sharp underwater spines
exercise: Why do carnivorous plants usually grow in bogs? | The soil there has very little nitrogen, so insects make up for it | Bogs are full of insects and nothing else, so catching them is easier | The water in bogs is too salty for roots to drink | Bogs have no sunlight, so they must eat
exercise: Where do carnivorous plants get most of their food? | From sunlight, like other green plants | From the insects they catch | From the mud around their roots | From the rainwater in their traps
exercise: About how many kinds of carnivorous plants are there? | Over 600 | Only 3 | About 20 | Over a million
exercise: How does a butterwort catch flies? | With flat sticky leaves | With a snapping trap like a jaw | With an underwater bladder | With a deep slippery pitcher
exercise: What does a Venus flytrap on a windowsill need? | Bright sun, wet soil and rainwater | Shade, dry soil and little bits of meat | Tap water and a warm room all winter | Plant food and a small burger every week
exercise: Why does the titan arum smell of rotting meat? | To bring in flies that pollinate it | To catch and eat the flies that land on its petals | To scare away hungry animals that might eat it | Because its whole flower is rotting away
test: «saw a cartoon plant swallow a whole man, that's real somewhere innit» What is right? | No, no plant eats people; the biggest pitchers catch at most a frog, a lizard or a rare rat | Yes, in the jungles of Madagascar, as explorers have reported | Yes, but only at night | No, but they do eat dogs and cats
test: In a video game a piranha plant pops out of a pipe and bites the hero. What is true of real carnivorous plants? | They catch small animals by traps and do not chase or bite anyone | They hide in pipes and jump out at people | They can bite, but only through thick gloves | They chew their food with tiny teeth
test: Count them: 3 flies are caught in a pitcher plant. How many legs went in? | 18 | 12 | 24 | 9
test: What would happen if you watered a flytrap with tap water every day? | Minerals in the tap water would slowly harm it | It would grow twice as fast as before and flower sooner | It would stop needing insects | Nothing at all, any water is the same
test: «gave my flytrap a bit of burger, it's a meat eater so it'll love it» What is best? | No, it cannot digest fat, and the trap may rot | Yes, a burger is its perfect food, full of meat | Yes, but only if the burger is cooked | No, flytraps eat only vegetables
test: A man in the 1800s read in a newspaper about a tree in Madagascar that ate people. What was it really? | A made-up hoax story | A real tree that is now extinct | A giant pitcher plant | A pet tiger hiding in a tree
test: Mia blows gently on a flytrap trap once. What happens? | Probably nothing, because it needs two trigger touches | It snaps shut at once and starts to digest | It dies straight away from the shock of being touched | It starts digesting the air
test: Why might a carnivorous plant help a bog plant more than a garden plant? | Bog soil has little nitrogen, and insects bring some in | Garden plants are too lazy to catch insects for food | Insects only live near bogs | Bog plants have no leaves for sunlight
test: «darwin fed plants?? sounds made up» What is right? | It is true; he tested sundews with bits of meat and hair | It is a joke from an old cartoon about a hungry plant | He fed them only bread and milk | It was his cat that fed them
test: In winter a flytrap kept in a warm, bright room never rests. What is likely? | It weakens, because it needs a cold winter rest | It grows bigger than ever and flowers all through the winter | It turns into a pitcher plant | It starts catching mice
test: Why is a bladderwort trap like a vacuum cleaner? | It lowers the pressure inside, then sucks prey in when the door opens | It makes a loud noise under the water to scare the prey towards it | It blows insects away from the plant | It runs on electricity from the pond
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a Venus flytrap requires two stimuli of its trigger hairs prior to closure. | The avoidance of wasteful closure upon raindrops and other non-living objects | The provision of time for the prey to escape | The necessity of warming the trap by sunlight before any movement is possible | The requirement to attract a second insect
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nutrition of carnivorous plants. | They manufacture their principal food by photosynthesis and derive additional nutrients from captured insects | They derive all of their food exclusively from captured insects, being unable to make any themselves | They obtain their food solely from the mud of bogs | They are incapable of photosynthesis
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reports of man-eating trees in Madagascar published during the nineteenth century? | They were a hoax without foundation in fact | They described a species now extinct | They were accurate accounts of giant pitcher plants | They referred to a carnivorous fungus
exam: Ascertain the mechanism by which a bladderwort captures its prey. | The rapid suction of water fleas into a low-pressure underwater bladder | The adhesion of flies to sticky tentacles above the surface of the water | The drowning of insects in a lidded pitcher | The snapping closure of a hinged leaf upon contact
